                              THE BLADE



    The man staggered back from his desk with both hands clapped
over his ears. Even from the fourth floor I saw it: the way his whole
body folded. He went down out of the frame like somebody pulled his
legs out.
    I took my hand off the pipe.
    The silence that followed was the loudest of my life.
    I was across Jerome Avenue before I’d put on shoes. I remember a
cab honking. I remember the netting snagging my sleeve. I remember
the lobby door of that building had a padlock that somebody had cut
open and then forgotten about, and I remember taking the stairs three
at a time, which my body has never done, and hearing my own heart in
the stairwell like a drum nobody had told to stop.
    On the third floor, the one open door.
    It was a bare room with a folding table, two monitors, a space heat‐
er, a cup of instant ramen, and a library copy of Fundamentals of Acous‐
tics open face-down on the sill. A kid sat on the floor against the wall
with blood running from his left nostril and a thin line of it from his
right ear. He was maybe twenty-two. He had a lanyard that said
DANNY, FIELD ACOUSTICS (CONTRACT), and headphones around
his neck, and one hand pressed hard to the side of his head.
    “Don’t,” he said. His voice had a wobble in it, like it was coming up
through water. “Don’t touch me, I can’t, my ear—”
    “I’m not. I’m not going to.” I stopped in the doorway with my hands
out where he could see them. “I’m so sorry. I’m so sorry. I’m—”
    He stared at me.
    “Wait,” he said. “Are you the guy with the projector?”
    I said yes. It seemed like the least useful thing I’d ever said.
    “Oh my God.” He laughed, a cracked, awful sound, and winced.
“I’ve been listening to you for seven weeks. The guy before me asked
for a transfer. You talk to a pipe.”



